# 5 Processes Stealing 10+ Hours a Week From Your Team

We've run enough audits to see the pattern. It's almost always the same five processes.

Individually, none of them looks like a problem. Twenty minutes here, forty there. That's the whole reason they survive — no single one is ever embarrassing enough to force a decision.

Add them up and something else appears. Usually 10 to 15 hours a week. Sometimes 30. In staff cost, that's routinely a full-time salary being paid to produce nothing that compounds.

Here's what we find, what it costs, and what to do about each.

## 1. Manual Check-In and Attendance Tracking

Someone stands at a desk or a gate and verifies people one at a time. Tickets, memberships, appointments, deliveries.

**What it costs:** three people at twenty minutes each per event or shift is sixty minutes of staff time — around **₦6,500 a day**, or roughly **₦1.6M a year** for a process that runs daily.

**The real cost isn't the wage, though.** It's the queue. We saw an event client running twelve gate agents. Attendees waited, staff absorbed the frustration, and the only output was "this person is legitimately allowed in."

QR check-in settles that in 0.4 seconds per attendee with zero gate staff. We built **Veripasshub** for exactly this — 50,000+ verified records and a real-time attendance dashboard. It wasn't a cost-cutting exercise. The gate simply stopped being a place where events went to lose momentum.

## 2. Spreadsheet-Based Scheduling

Bookings live in a spreadsheet, then get copied into a calendar, then reconciled by hand when two people book the same slot.

**What it costs:** double-bookings, no-shows, and roughly **two hours a day** of an ops manager's attention spent keeping two sources of truth roughly aligned.

**What fixes it:** a single system that owns the booking. Self-service booking for the customer, automatic conflict prevention, reminders that reduce no-shows before they cost you anything.

This is the first thing we automated in [Gym House](https://gym-house-gules.vercel.app), and it was the least glamorous. It just removed the daily reconciliation nobody was hired to do.

## 3. Data Entry and Reconciliation

The same information exists in three places and someone makes them agree. Payments against invoices, orders against inventory, forms against a database.

**What it costs:** the most expensive kind of error. Manual entry runs a **1–4% error rate** — on 10,000 rows, that's up to 400 mistakes, each one needing a human to find it.

**What fixes it:** one system that writes once and propagates. Not a faster person. A structure where the duplicate entry can't happen.

## 4. Manual Invoicing and Payment Follow-Ups

An invoice gets generated, emailed, then chased. Then chased again. Then chased a third time.

**What it costs:** the invoice itself is minutes. The *chasing* is the job — and it's weeks of scattered attention for a single outcome: money that was already owed.

**What fixes it:** automatic generation on trigger, a payment link in the same message, and follow-ups on a schedule. We watched a logistics client move invoicing from two days to two minutes. The money arrived faster too, which is the part people underestimate.

## 5. Reporting — Pulling From Five Places Into One Spreadsheet

Every Monday, someone assembles last week's picture from five systems by hand.

**What it costs:** three to four hours weekly, and worse — the report is always about last week. You're managing a business with a five-to-nine day reporting lag, which means problems surface long after they were cheap to fix.

**What fixes it:** a scheduled job that aggregates and emails a digest. The same output, every Monday at 7am, with no human involved.

## The Ordering Rule

Don't start with the most impressive one. Start with the one that repeats most often and costs the most per cycle.

That's the whole discipline. A repeating loop is the easiest thing to automate and the payback math is the cleanest — you can see exactly how many cycles it takes to cover the build.

